<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akşehir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Philomelium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akşehir is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> Province in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mountains, ancient Philomelium. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and the Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> it was an important stop on the road between Konya and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the 14th century. The Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> died there in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s captivity in 1403, and it holds the tomb of Nasreddin Hodja.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aksehir.jpg" alt="Akşehir" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nedim Ardoğa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ak%C5%9Fehir_Ethnography_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
